\title{nitRo: Find and Fix Slow or Unsafe Patterns in R Code}
\description{
Analyses R code for common performance problems and unsafe idioms without running it, using a registry of pattern-matching rules that cover base R, vector allocation, loops, 'dplyr', 'data.table', file input and output, string handling, arithmetic and parallel processing. Rules whose rewrite gives identical results, such as replacing apply(m, 1, sum) with rowSums(m), are applied automatically. The original and rewritten code can then be run side by side to check that their results are identical and to compare their speed with 'microbenchmark'.
}
\details{
The main function is \code{\link[=nitro]{nitro()}}, which analyses an R expression. Use
\code{\link[=nitro_file]{nitro_file()}} for a script and \code{\link[=list_rules]{list_rules()}} to see every rule.

Each rule has a severity:
\describe{
\item{error_prone}{The pattern can give wrong results or errors in
some cases, for example \code{1:length(x)} when \code{x} is empty.}
\item{warning}{The pattern is measurably slower than an equivalent.}
\item{suggestion}{An alternative may be faster, depending on the data
or the context.}
\item{style}{Readability or robustness; no effect on speed.}
}

Only rules whose rewrite gives identical results are fixed
automatically; the others are reported with an explanation.
